22:46:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: You can’t see the flag on the Moon with even the most powerful telescopes because of a fundamental limit called **angular resolution**—the ability to distinguish small details at a distance. Here’s why that matters: - **The flag is tiny compared to the Moon’s distance.** The Apollo flags were about 1.2 meters (4 feet) wide. The Moon is roughly 384,400 kilometers (239,000 miles) away. At that distance, the flag’s angular size is only about **0.0007 arcseconds**. To put that in perspective, that’s like trying to see a human hair from 10 kilometers away. - **Telescopes have a resolution limit set by their aperture size.** A telescope’s resolving power depends on the diameter of its mirror or lens and the wavelength of light. Even the Hubble Space Telescope, with its 2.4-meter mirror, has a maximum resolution of about **0.05 arcseconds** under ideal conditions. That corresponds to roughly 90 meters (300 feet) on the lunar surface—nowhere near enough to pick out a 1.2-meter flag. - **Atmospheric distortion and other factors make it worse.** Ground-based telescopes, no matter how large, are further limited by Earth’s atmosphere, which blurs fine details. Adaptive optics help, but they still can’t overcome the sheer distance. So, while the flag is still there (in one piece or not), it’s simply far too small for any existing telescope to resolve. To see it, you’d need a telescope with a mirror dozens of meters across—or just send a probe back to the Moon to take a close-up picture, as NASA’s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter has already done from orbit.
